    "first": {
      "candidate_id": "symbolic-001",
      "parent_id": "symbolic-000",
      "source_sha256": "a3c5cb82c24b765aab52ceb63487ccb7db9fb255807a3ffc817ad99df6357619",
      "origin": "model_proposed",
      "n_params": 3,
      "rationale": "The incumbent x + v + x*x achieves NMSE=0.0964 with almost no curvature signal in x (x-bin means oscillate around zero with no monotone pattern). However, the v-binned RMSE is sharply non-uniform: the highest-v bin has RMSE=0.0322 versus 0.012-0.019 in the other three v-bins, and its mean residual is the largest (0.0017). This indicates amplitude-dependent structure in velocity - large |v| needs a stronger restoring/damping contribution than a purely linear v term supplies. A physically natural form is a quadratic-in-v contribution (e.g., quadratic damping). Replacing x*x with v*v keeps parameter count at 3, allows least squares to fit the observed high-v tail, and addresses the diagnostic evidence the incumbent's x*x term failed to address. Least squares can zero the coefficient if v*v adds nothing, so the model is no worse than linear if the signal is truly absent; if it helps, NMSE should drop noticeably since the worst residual band is concentrated where v*v is large.",
      "strategy_version": "paired-compact-feedback-v0.1",
